### Security Guidelines

#### Input Validation
- **Validate all inputs**: Sanitize and validate user inputs
- **URL validation**: Properly validate URLs and file paths
- **Data sanitization**: Sanitize data before processing
  ```typescript
  ✅ function validateDownloadUrl(url: string): boolean {
    try {
      const parsedUrl = new URL(url);
      const allowedProtocols = ['http:', 'https:'];
      
      if (!allowedProtocols.includes(parsedUrl.protocol)) {
        return false;
      }
      
      // Additional validation logic
      return isValidDomain(parsedUrl.hostname);
    } catch {
      return false;
    }
  }
  
  ✅ function sanitizeFileName(fileName: string): string {
    return fileName
      .replace(/[<>:"/\\|?*]/g, '') // Remove invalid characters
      .trim()
      .substring(0, 255); // Limit length
  }
  
  ❌ // No validation
  function processUrl(url) {
    return fetch(url); // Dangerous without validation
  }
  ```

#### Permission Model
- **Request permissions**: Explicitly request needed permissions
- **Principle of least privilege**: Only request minimum required permissions
- **Permission documentation**: Document why permissions are needed
  ```typescript
  ✅ // In manifest.json
  {
    "permissions": [
      "downloads:read",
      "downloads:write",
      "filesystem:read"
    ],
    "permissionReason": {
      "downloads:write": "Required to add custom download sources",
      "filesystem:read": "Needed to read format conversion templates"
    }
  }
  
  ✅ // Permission checking
  async function performAction(api: PluginAPI) {
    if (!await api.permissions.hasPermission('downloads:write')) {
      throw new Error('Insufficient permissions for this action');
    }
    
    // Proceed with action
  }
  
  ❌ // Overly broad permissions
  "permissions": ["*"] // Too permissive
  ```

### Performance Best Practices

#### Resource Management
- **Memory efficiency**: Manage memory usage carefully
- **Async operations**: Use async operations for I/O
- **Resource cleanup**: Clean up resources on unload
  ```typescript
  ✅ class PluginResourceManager {
    private resources: Set<Disposable> = new Set();
    private timers: Set<NodeJS.Timeout> = new Set();
    
    addResource(resource: Disposable): void {
      this.resources.add(resource);
    }
    
    addTimer(timer: NodeJS.Timeout): void {
      this.timers.add(timer);
    }
    
    async cleanup(): Promise<void> {
      // Clear timers
      this.timers.forEach(timer => clearTimeout(timer));
      this.timers.clear();
      
      // Dispose resources
      await Promise.all(
        Array.from(this.resources).map(resource => resource.dispose())
      );
      this.resources.clear();
    }
  }
  
  ❌ // No resource management
  setInterval(() => {
    // Long-running timer without cleanup
  }, 1000);
  ```

#### Efficient Processing
- **Batch operations**: Batch API calls when possible
- **Debounce frequent operations**: Use debouncing for frequent updates
- **Lazy loading**: Load resources only when needed
  ```typescript
  ✅ // Debounced processing
  const debouncedProcessDownloads = debounce(async (downloads: Download[]) => {
    await processDownloadBatch(downloads);
  }, 500);
  
  ✅ // Lazy loading
  async function getFormatConverter(): Promise<FormatConverter> {
    if (!this.converter) {
      this.converter = await import('./format-converter');
    }
    return this.converter;
  }
  
  ❌ // Inefficient processing
  downloads.forEach(async download => {
    await api.processDownload(download); // Sequential processing
  });
  ```

### Error Handling and Logging

#### Comprehensive Error Handling
- **Try-catch blocks**: Use try-catch for error-prone operations
- **Error classification**: Classify different types of errors
- **Recovery strategies**: Implement recovery where possible
  ```typescript
  ✅ class PluginErrorHandler {
    async handleOperation<T>(
      operation: () => Promise<T>,
      context: string
    ): Promise<T | null> {
      try {
        return await operation();
      } catch (error) {
        this.logError(error, context);
        
        if (this.isRecoverable(error)) {
          return await this.attemptRecovery(operation, context);
        }
        
        throw new PluginError(
          `${context} failed: ${error.message}`,
          error.code || 'UNKNOWN_ERROR'
        );
      }
    }
    
    private logError(error: Error, context: string): void {
      console.error(`[Plugin Error] ${context}:`, {
        message: error.message,
        stack: error.stack,
        timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
      });
    }
  }
  
  ❌ // Poor error handling
  async function riskyOperation() {
    const result = await fetch(url); // No error handling
    return result.json(); // Could throw
  }
  ```

#### Logging Best Practices
- **Structured logging**: Use structured log formats
- **Appropriate log levels**: Use correct log levels
- **No sensitive data**: Don't log sensitive information
  ```typescript
  ✅ class PluginLogger {
    info(message: string, data?: Record<string, unknown>): void {
      console.log(`[${this.pluginId}] INFO: ${message}`, data);
    }
    
    warn(message: string, data?: Record<string, unknown>): void {
      console.warn(`[${this.pluginId}] WARN: ${message}`, data);
    }
    
    error(message: string, error?: Error, data?: Record<string, unknown>): void {
      console.error(`[${this.pluginId}] ERROR: ${message}`, {
        error: error?.message,
        stack: error?.stack,
        ...data
      });
    }
  }
  
  ❌ // Poor logging
  console.log('Something happened', sensitiveUserData); // Don't log sensitive data
  ```

## Exceptions
- **Legacy plugin compatibility**: May require different patterns
- **Performance-critical plugins**: May need optimized approaches
- **Platform-specific functionality**: May require platform-specific code
- **Development and testing**: May have relaxed security for development

## Enforcement
- Plugin validation during loading
- API usage monitoring
- Security audits for plugins
- Performance monitoring and limits

